<<~ ahu #/entry>>

! Mandate → Activity → Appraisal

''The agency cut.'' A human, an AI process, a vessel, and an outcome may all participate in one act without becoming one identity. This membrane names the relations so the causal graph carries the distinction.

''Mandate'' is the content-addressed intent an actor authors or delegates. ''Activity'' is one concrete execution attempt. ''Result'' is the entity the activity generates, including a receipt for an external effect. ''Delegation'' relates a responsible actor to a bounded delegate for a named activity. ''Attestation'' is an integrity-bound claim about a subject and activity. ''Appraisal'' is this causal island's local decision over the evidence and capabilities it can presently hold.

The mandate does not become the activity. The AI process does not become the human author. A signature proves who made an attestation; it does not by itself prove permission or pono. Local appraisal decides.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/worldline-boundary>>

!! Worldline boundary

An ephemeral tasked spirit remains ''nameless'' in local work memory: its derived worldline handle, role, task and held capability stack locate its work. A spirit acquires an explicit process identity only when an activity crosses an authority, vessel, or federation boundary that needs a separately attributable principal. Agent cryptographic fingerprinting of every local helper is deferred; the local graph must not invent identity material merely to make a pet-name look official.

The boundary record carries the parent mandate, activity, result, delegation relation and local appraisal. Re-delegation is closed by default and must carry an explicit attenuating grant; a child never widens the parent's envelope.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/appraisal>>

!! Local appraisal is a gradient

Every vessel MAY preserve the whole evidence graph while returning a bounded local verdict:

* ''accepted'' — the evidence and held capabilities satisfy local policy;
* ''deferred'' — evidence is usable but a human or later local ceremony must decide;
* ''unsettled'' — admissible concurrent heads contradict one another;
* ''unavailable'' — a required parent, authority, or evidence is absent;
* ''rejected'' — a subject, signature, capability, or relation fails its structural gate.

These are local appraisals, never a global present. Arrival order, wall-clock freshness, schedule order, pet-name, persona, and vessel class MUST NOT choose among causal heads.

''Schedule law.'' A scheduler triggers an activity against a fixed mandate CID. At execution the vessel re-appraises that mandate against its locally visible causal frontier and held cap stack. A schedule never means ''latest'', and a timer never grants authority.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/scenario-harness>>

!! Red-first ScenarioCast guidance

`ScenarioPresence` describes who and what caps stand in the scenario. It does not stand in for an activity. The harness SHOULD compose these pure records:

```
ScenarioPresence   → actor + vessel caps + descriptive hats
ScenarioMandate    → authored/delegated intent entity
ScenarioActivity   → one execution with causal parents
ScenarioResult     → generated subject or external-effect receipt
ScenarioAttestation→ issuer-bound claim about that subject
ScenarioAppraisal  → local accepted/deferred/unsettled/unavailable/rejected
```

An AI process enters the cast as its own actor descriptor when it crosses the explicit-process boundary; it is never merely a human `AI` hat. Tests MUST prove that labels do not mint authority, a result cannot use an absent mandate parent, a valid signature on the wrong subject is rejected, concurrent contradictory activities remain unsettled in either arrival order, and re-delegation cannot widen capabilities.

The fiction failure modes are test names, not authority: False Archive tests record/inference separation; Approval-Rot tests contrary evidence; Unsanctioned Expansion tests mandate scope; Context-Fade tests fixed constraints under long runs; Signal-Blur tests epistemic labels; Persona-Capture tests that ritual framing cannot authorize an action.

<<~/ahu>>

